## What Happened

`DSA.Create()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` on macOS because Apple's native cryptographic framework (Security.framework / CryptoKit) **does not support DSA**. Microsoft has been deprecating DSA since .NET 7 (diagnostic `SYSLIB0028`), and in .NET 11 the macOS implementation was removed entirely — the platform shim that previously bridged to OpenSSL is no longer included.

On Linux and Windows, OS-level DSA support still exists (OpenSSL / CNG), so those platforms continue to work.

## How to Fix

You have two options:

### Option 1: Use BouncyCastle for legacy verification (recommended)

```csharp
// dotnet add package BouncyCastle.Cryptography
using Org.BouncyCastle.Crypto;
using Org.BouncyCastle.Security;

public bool VerifyLegacyDsa(byte[] data, byte[] signature, byte[] publicKeyBlob)
{
    var publicKey = PublicKeyFactory.CreateKey(publicKeyBlob);
    var signer = SignerUtilities.GetSigner("SHA1withDSA");
    signer.Init(forSigning: false, publicKey);
    signer.BlockUpdate(data, 0, data.Length);
    return signer.VerifySignature(signature);
}
```

This is cross-platform and doesn't depend on OS crypto libraries.

### Option 2: Conditionally use OpenSSL on macOS

Ship OpenSSL via Homebrew or bundle it, and P/Invoke directly — but this adds deployment complexity and is fragile.

## Summary

| | Linux | Windows | macOS |
|---|---|---|---|
| .NET 10 | ✅ OpenSSL | ✅ CNG | ⚠️ Worked via shim |
| .NET 11 | ✅ OpenSSL | ✅ CNG | ❌ Removed |

**Recommendation:** Migrate to BouncyCastle for DSA verification. It's portable, maintained, and avoids relying on deprecated OS support. For new signatures, migrate to **Ed25519** or **ECDSA**.
